  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once with status `ok` and no errors.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) is web server access logs. Each record has:\n- **Request details:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`, `host`\n- **Outcome:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text with a `.keyword` copy) and `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `tags`, plus a computed `hour_of_day` field\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-9f195c6c Web logs overview\"** (id `8f803401-25ec-46f0-85e7-fc36cd50ff8b`, default time range last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- **Four headline numbers:** total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average response size in bytes, and the count of server errors (5xx).\n- **Requests over time:** a stacked bar chart split by response code.\n- **Requests by response code:** a donut chart.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** a table with request count and total bytes.\n- **Top 10 source countries:** a table with request count and unique visitors.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-9f195c6c 5xx spike\"** (id `aa0fc09f-efed-4ff3-856c-4f169f698c82`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It counts requests whose `response.keyword` is from 500 up to (not including) 600 over the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`. It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\n- **It only notifies inside Kibana.** I didn't attach any actions, so it won't send email, Slack messages or anything else. If you want notifications, tell me which connector to use.\n- **The 5xx check compares codes as text.** Both the rule and the dashboard's server-errors panel treat 5xx as anything from \"500\" to \"599\" alphabetically, because the code is stored as text. That works because every status code is three digits.",
